// @check
// @error-at 26:25 'padLeft' is not imported — a free `padLeft(self: …)` accepting string is exported by "std:fmt"; a UFCS call resolves only names in scope, so import `padLeft` from there
// D1230 — THE MODULE IS IN THE GRAPH BUT NOT IN THIS FILE'S IMPORTS, and the
// suggestion is still exact.
//
// The entry imports only `./dep`; `./dep` is what imports `std:fmt`. So the
// quick-fix has no existing `import … from "std:fmt"` statement to extend — it
// has to INSERT one — and the specifier it inserts has to be right from HERE.
//
// A `std:` key is what makes that safe: `modResolveSpecifier` returns a `std:`
// specifier unchanged from every file, so the text is identical wherever it is
// written. A RELATIVE module the entry does not import gets NO suggestion, and
// deliberately: its key is a normalized path, and turning that back into a
// specifier relative to this file is `..`-arithmetic that can name a different
// module. A withheld suggestion costs a reader the old sentence; a wrong one
// costs them an import that resolves somewhere else.
//
// THE RECEIVER IS A STRING, which is also the second raise site. A string
// receiver never reaches `checkMemberNode` — the call arm answers `no method
// '.padLeft' on string` and returns — so the enrichment is wired there too. Its
// control is `tests/cases/strings/ufcs-no-such-method.vl`, the same call in a
// program with no module graph at all.
import { tag } from "./dep"

print(tag(3))

print("ab".padLeft(5, "."))
